    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d89">

                <pb facs="0213" n="101" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg101"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.51693 Manchuria/6: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cunningham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-04-01T13:00:00+08:00">April 1, 1935—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 1—12:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>152. 1. My 148, March 30, 11 a.m.<anchor n="96" xml:id="d89-f1"/> I have received
                    following letter from Shanghai branch National City Bank signed by J. A. Macar,
                    Assistant Vice President: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“With reference to your recent inquiry concerning the closing of our
                            Mukden branch to become effective May 31, 1935, we wish to advise that
                            since the major portion of our Manchurian business always has been in
                            Harbin it was felt that internal efficiency warranted concentration on
                            that branch.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <pb facs="0214" n="102" xml:id="pg102"/>
                <p>Andersen, Meyer and Company have verbally informed this office that in effect
                    their Mukden branch was closed because of inability to meet Japanese
                    competition.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
